teach.body:
A thorough laboratory evaluation must precede surgical evacuation:

Complete blood count to quantify microcytic anaemia resulting from recurrent uterine bleeding;

Blood group and Rhesus antibody screen to identify Rh-negative women requiring anti-D immunoglobulin;

Thyroid function tests (TSH, free T4) to detect biochemical or clinical hyperthyroidism driven by massive hCG cross-reactivity;

Serum creatinine, electrolytes, liver function tests, and coagulation profile (PT, INR, aPTT, fibrinogen) to assess baseline organ function.

A pre-evacuation chest radiograph with abdominal shielding is mandatory to exclude pulmonary metastases and provide a baseline for pulmonary embolisation.

Gestational trophoblastic neoplasia is diagnosed during post-molar serial hCG surveillance when any of the following international FIGO criteria are met:

1. An hCG plateau across four consecutive weekly values over at least three weeks (days 1, 7, 14, and 21);

2. An hCG rise of ten percent or greater across three consecutive weekly values over at least two weeks (days 1, 7, and 14);

3. Persistence of detectable serum beta-hCG six months or more following surgical evacuation;

4. A histological diagnosis of choriocarcinoma.
